WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:14.940
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.823
Welcomes to "Eyes
On Enterprise,"

00:00:16.823 --> 00:00:18.240
where I'll be
bringing on Googlers

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.970
to talk about how the technology
landscapes are changing

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.580
how enterprises adapt,
modernize, and scale.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.650
My name is Stephanie
Wong, developer advocate.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.650
And today, I have Yufueng Guo,
developer advocate for machine

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:29.650
learning on the show.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:30.760
Welcome to the show.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:31.620
YUFUENG GUO: Thanks
so much, Stephanie.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.312
I'm really excited
to be here and chat

00:00:33.312 --> 00:00:34.270
about machine learning.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.062
STEPHANIE WONG: So
first off, just thinking

00:00:36.062 --> 00:00:37.560
about some examples
of it, do you

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:41.250
have any that you've encountered
of specific ML use cases that

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.950
have come in and kind
of swept a company off

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.130
its feet a little bit?

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.230
YUFUENG GUO: Well,
the first kind

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:52.260
of big examples around these
came from image recognition

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.400
type use cases.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.060
So on the one hand,
you have com examples

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.430
like Google Photos
really changing

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:01.650
how we think about
storing and searching

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:05.069
for pictures and other
kinds of recognition tasks

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:07.890
where you can auto tag images.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.660
And then of course,
medicine is now

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.290
seeing a rapid
shift in diagnosis

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:16.500
in radiology, where they're
applying the technology

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.470
to find things more
accurately, and more quickly,

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.780
and more consistently
than doctors can,

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.907
because machines don't fatigue,
and they're always consistent.

00:01:26.907 --> 00:01:29.490
They're not going to be upset,
because they were late for work

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.500
and stuck in traffic
earlier that morning.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.070
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.570
And one thing I was
thinking about earlier

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.040
was machine learning versus AI.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.707
I think there's a lot
of confusion there

00:01:36.707 --> 00:01:38.520
about which term is
technically accurate.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.380
So what's your opinion on that?

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.270
YUFUENG GUO: Classically
or traditionally,

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.950
AI was referring to the
broad field of just general

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.780
artificial intelligence AI.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:54.030
And it was about systems that
were artificial intelligence,

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:57.910
and anything really
could apply to that.

00:01:57.910 --> 00:01:59.880
Decades ago in the
'80s, a lot of these

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.085
were rule-based systems.

00:02:01.085 --> 00:02:02.460
So there were
things that behaved

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.940
like an intelligence would,
at least how back then, folks

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:06.877
thought about it.

00:02:06.877 --> 00:02:08.460
But they were just
rule-based systems,

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:09.970
if statements, and
things like that.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:14.130
If this happens, then we'll do
this, and not terribly complex,

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:14.700
right?

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.390
But there were lots of different
approaches to doing AI.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.295
So AI's kind of
the broad umbrella.

00:02:20.295 --> 00:02:21.300
STEPHANIE WONG: Got you.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.980
YUFUENG GUO: Machine learning
then becomes one set of tools

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.250
to try to achieve
AI, and it's possible

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.430
that we'll ultimately kind
kind of achieve or do better

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.710
in AI with some other set
of tools that isn't even

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.572
machine learning or maybe
evolves from machine learning.

00:02:37.572 --> 00:02:39.030
And so a machine
learning is really

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.500
about some mixture of
statistical inference and kind

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.740
of training on some set of
data, and creating a model,

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:48.480
and doing these predictions.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:49.080
STEPHANIE WONG:
Yeah, that's great.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.610
I think there used a lot of
the times interchangeably.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:53.280
But it's great
clarification to have,

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.560
because they really are
quite different in what

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:56.280
they encompass.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:57.000
YUFUENG GUO: Absolutely.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:57.180
STEPHANIE WONG: All right.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.953
So let's talk about a typical
use case for an enterprise.

00:02:59.953 --> 00:03:01.620
For a company that's
just looking to get

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.495
started, dip their toes
into machine learning

00:03:03.495 --> 00:03:05.640
for the first time,
what would you

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.670
describe as the workflow
for them to get started?

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.710
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah,
so assuming that they

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.950
have some kind of system
they're trying to model,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:16.330
some kind of task
they have in mind,

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:18.180
the first consideration
then becomes

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:19.900
checking to make sure
they have the data.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.420
And if not,
collecting that data.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.440
And so for example, if
they want to figure out

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.180
consumer preferences, they
say they're a retail store.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.210
And so they'll collect that
data around kind of purchasing

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.460
patterns and things like that.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:35.040
And many retailers
these days probably

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.860
have that data already that's
sitting around somewhere.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.152
They've got to figure out
how to get their hands on it,

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:42.660
because it might be siloed
off, in different parts

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.160
of their organization,
and there are

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.410
people who might be
holding on closely to that.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:49.890
And so getting access to that
in a way where you can aggregate

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:51.930
data together is important.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.350
And then once you
have that data,

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:54.670
you can do analysis on it.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:56.550
So that becomes kind
of the next piece,

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.550
looking at what data do
you have, is it useful,

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.140
are their signals in it?

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.590
And from there, you can do
kind of the traditional machine

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.510
learning training piece of
it, where you take that data,

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:11.110
train up a model, and then
deploy it to make predictions.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.332
But we're not quite done yet.

00:04:13.332 --> 00:04:14.790
Just because you've
created a model

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.339
and put it in production
doesn't mean that you can now

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:21.120
forget about it, and just let it
sit there, just as you wouldn't

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.420
leave production code out on
your website or its system

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:25.660
and just say, I'm done.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.540
We don't need software
developers anymore.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:28.822
This software is done.

00:04:28.822 --> 00:04:30.030
It's just going to sit there.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:31.270
That's not going to work.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.170
And so models need to be
updated over time as well.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.560
As purchasing patterns
change with the seasons,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.560
with the years, that
information should feed back

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.103
and inform updated models.

00:04:42.103 --> 00:04:43.020
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.090
So content evaluation
of the model.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.490
And then as things change,
you need to incorporate that

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:48.240
into the training.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:49.115
YUFUENG GUO: Exactly.

00:04:49.115 --> 00:04:50.550
So it's kind of,
in a lot of ways,

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:51.970
just like software development.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:54.460
You need to maintain
your model over time.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:54.900
STEPHANIE WONG:
So I want to dive

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.040
into that a little
bit more, because I

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.665
know that you had a
video that was very

00:04:57.665 --> 00:05:00.960
popular a couple years ago that
was on the "7 Steps to Machine

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.870
Learning," and that included
gathering your data, data

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:07.830
preparation, choosing a
model, training, evaluation,

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:10.840
and hyperparameter tuning,
and then prediction as well.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.200
So can you tell me about each
of the steps a little bit

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.080
in detail?

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.000
Who do you hire
for each of those?

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.300
And what are the trade-offs,
a little bit more about that?

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:20.050
YUFUENG GUO: Sure.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:23.870
So with these seven
steps, so to speak,

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:27.010
there's no real, seven
steps, six steps, 10 steps.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.350
You can break it out
however you'd like.

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:32.410
It was just really one
way to delineate it.

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.570
And a lot of times,
different steps

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.350
can be collapsed
into one job role.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.900
It could be one week's worth
of work where you gather data,

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.745
and you kind of do that
data preparation, cleaning,

00:05:43.745 --> 00:05:44.620
and things like that.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:45.578
That might go together.

00:05:45.578 --> 00:05:47.170
You might end up
making pipeline.

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:49.120
Whereas new data
comes in, and you know

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:51.320
how you'd like to transform it.

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:53.230
You can set up an
ongoing streaming

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:56.230
job where that data coming
in gets transformed and then

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:59.210
deposited in some kind
of data warehouse.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:02.080
Then looking onwards to
things like training and model

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:04.120
development as well
as you alluded to,

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:05.440
hyperparameter tuning.

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:08.860
This is really the meat of
it, where a lot of people

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:12.790
spend a lot of their kind
of mental energy thinking

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:14.860
about modeling.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:19.900
In a lot of ways, especially
for known use cases and kind

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:23.890
of solved problems that
aren't research bleeding edge

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:27.370
situations, you can use a lot
of existing state-of-the-art

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:30.640
models and adapt them for
your particular data set,

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:34.030
customize it, and get really
pretty much state-of-the-art

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:35.590
results for your data set.

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:36.520
STEPHANIE WONG: So I want
to go back to a point

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:38.770
that you mentioned earlier
about model evaluation.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.980
Can you talk about why it's so
important for an organization

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:44.380
to consistently
evaluate their model?

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:46.300
Are there any use
cases that showcase

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.700
that it's so crucial or
failures that you've seen?

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:49.450
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah.

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:52.510
So we can take kind of a toy
example and work through it.

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:53.250
Right.

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:57.970
The data that you
are predicting on

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.640
needs to come from the
same world, so to speak,

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:02.980
that you've trained
the model on.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.680
So this, in statistical
terms, means

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.200
your training, evaluation
tests, and all this data

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:11.500
needs to come from the same
distribution, essentially.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.740
And furthermore,
that distribution

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:17.800
also needs to reflect the
reality of the world, right?

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:19.340
So there's two pieces to it.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:21.340
For example, if,
let's say, we go

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:24.040
and we train a model
that can recognize

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:26.810
pictures of lawn furniture.

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:29.980
So chairs, and then
you go in the studio.

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:32.118
And you can take--
you're a chair company.

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:33.910
You make lots of
different kinds of chairs.

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:35.440
So you're going to make a
model, and then you maybe

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.270
you make an app that
people can hold up

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:39.360
their camera to the chair,
and be like, oh, yes.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:40.360
It's this kind of chair.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.730
Now, I can go buy one for
my mind as well or something

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:43.450
like that.

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:45.347
And so you bring in--

00:07:45.347 --> 00:07:46.930
you set up a big
studio, and you bring

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:50.350
in all of your latest, hottest
chairs for the next season.

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:52.660
And you put it on nice
backdrops with good lighting,

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:54.690
and you take a bunch
of pictures and videos.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.020
And you do up a whole shoot.

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:59.230
And you take that
content, all those images,

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:01.270
and you train a machine
learning model that

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:03.110
can recognize these images.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:06.760
This is great if your task
that you're trying to make

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:09.940
is a model that can recognize
pictures of your products

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.070
in, say, your catalog, right?

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:14.423
Because those are the
same pictures basically,

00:08:14.423 --> 00:08:16.090
and they will be in
the same situations,

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:17.320
and lighting, and all that.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:20.140
But it doesn't really
help our actual use case

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:24.820
where they are going to be
in your customers' homes

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:28.575
with a backdrop of grass
and other-- the patio

00:08:28.575 --> 00:08:29.450
and things like that.

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:31.200
It will be in the
context of other things,

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.679
so you've got to capture
data in the same world

00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:36.280
that you're going
to be predicting on.

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:40.299
And furthermore, and this is
kind of the ongoing evaluation

00:08:40.299 --> 00:08:43.120
piece, what happens
when winter comes

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.370
and that green
grass turns white,

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:48.490
and your model's never
seen snow before?

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:50.270
It's going to start
having problems.

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:55.140
And so this is why you need
a diversity of training data

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:58.510
in all sorts of scenarios
that is representative

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:02.020
of how you expect your model
to be seeing these images

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:03.280
during prediction time.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.260
STEPHANIE WONG: So hence,
constant evaluation.

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:06.010
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah.

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:07.760
STEPHANIE WONG: And
then just as important

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:09.910
to collect data
points of labels that

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:12.310
are the prediction that
you're looking for, but also,

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:13.660
examples where it's not that.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:15.040
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah, absolutely.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:15.540
Yeah.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:17.860
So in this particular
scenario, it

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:22.240
would mean also having
images of these environments

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:23.890
with your chair removed.

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:24.807
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:09:24.807 --> 00:09:26.330
YUFUENG GUO: So
just a normal lawn.

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:27.450
Maybe there's some
leaves on the lawn.

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:29.110
STEPHANIE WONG: So of
course, the question arises,

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:31.450
what if the validation
performance of the model

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:32.500
just isn't up to par?

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:33.250
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah.

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:35.140
I mean, poor model
performance can

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:36.945
come from all sorts of factors.

00:09:36.945 --> 00:09:39.070
It includes things like
the raw modeling technique.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:41.890
Maybe you don't
have enough data.

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:44.330
Maybe you're over
fitting on that data.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:46.330
There's lots of possible
causes, and this

00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:49.330
is kind of where expertise
and experience really

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:50.330
become valuable.

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:54.490
It's easy to follow
the kind of golden path

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:57.070
of just do this, and do this,
and do this, and it'll work.

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:59.890
But then when something
goes wrong, fixing problems,

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:02.945
that's where expertise
comes through.

00:10:02.945 --> 00:10:03.820
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:04.890
And overfitting,
what do you-- can

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:05.992
you just describe what that is?

00:10:05.992 --> 00:10:06.742
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah.

00:10:06.742 --> 00:10:09.490
So overfitting is
essentially a way

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:11.410
to describe the fact
that the model has

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:13.760
memorized the training data.

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:16.760
This is bad, because it's
going to struggle them

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.610
with real predictions,
because all it knows

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:21.620
is the exact training there.

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:23.450
So in that example
with images, it's

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:26.060
basically memorized
these pictures and said,

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.250
I will only classify
this as a lawn chair

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:31.872
if I see this exact image
or these 10 exact images.

00:10:31.872 --> 00:10:33.830
STEPHANIE WONG: So let's
say I have my problem,

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:35.960
I've collected the
data, prepared it.

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:37.700
What do I do to set it up?

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:39.290
And what tooling
exists out there?

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:41.390
What's the general
developer experience

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:42.360
for machine learning?

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:45.060
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah, so for
developing your own model,

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:47.930
there's a lot of
libraries out there today,

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:50.210
things like TensorFlow
being a prominent one

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:53.098
in the industry that's really
seen a lot of adoption.

00:10:53.098 --> 00:10:54.890
Of course, there's
things like scikit-learn

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.580
which is, as I
mentioned earlier,

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:58.955
it's kind of a machine learning,
but not technically deep

00:10:58.955 --> 00:10:59.455
learning.

00:10:59.455 --> 00:11:00.740
It's deep learning adjacence.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:02.570
But also, machine
learning is a toolkit.

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:05.630
And of course, there's also
things like Keras, and PyTorch,

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:07.880
and other deep learning
tools that can also

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:12.200
be employed to model
a given data set,

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:14.180
and then in terms of
a language like AR

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:16.970
which is more targeted
toward statisticians

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:19.380
who have a lot more experience
with that sort of thing.

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:21.200
You can also use it
for structured data.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.420
Sometimes language models
will show up there,

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:27.590
but by and large, the industry
has seen this real shift

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:31.760
to using Python as
the language of choice

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:33.540
around doing machine learning.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:35.030
STEPHANIE WONG: So I want to
talk about this by problem

00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:36.947
type, because as you
said, a lot of industries

00:11:36.947 --> 00:11:38.783
have their own
styles of data sets,

00:11:38.783 --> 00:11:40.700
and they need to approach
it in the right way.

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:42.590
So can you talk about
how you would do that?

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:43.340
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah.

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:45.980
So I think the first question
you have to ask yourself

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:49.190
when you want to do some kind
of machine learning or data

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:52.550
science task is, is it
a descriptive problem?

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:54.530
Is it a predictive problem?

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:56.625
Or is a prescriptive problem?

00:11:56.625 --> 00:11:58.250
So I know those are
kind of long words,

00:11:58.250 --> 00:11:59.270
and they all kind
of sound similar,

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:01.320
so let's go into each
of those a little bit.

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:03.920
A descriptive problem
is really, kind

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:06.238
of your traditional classic
data analysis problem.

00:12:06.238 --> 00:12:08.780
A lot of times when people think
they need a machine learning

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:13.432
solution, really, they are
just asking given some data,

00:12:13.432 --> 00:12:15.140
can you help me find
some patterns in it?

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:16.940
I don't really know
what I'm looking for.

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:18.950
Can you just describe
some interesting things?

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:21.740
So dashboarding, putting up
some nice visualizations,

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:23.750
cleaning up the data so
that you can do that.

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:25.760
That is, in a lot of
ways, a good first step

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.920
to even doing other types
of machine learning tasks.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.170
So that's descriptive.

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:32.780
And then there's
predictive, which

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:35.920
is much of what we classically
think of as machine learning.

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:37.400
You have your training data.

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:39.800
There is something you're
trying to predict, right?

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:43.920
And then you try to model
and make these predictions.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:48.180
So that's something where you
have inputs in the real world.

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:51.500
And then you say, oh,
I predict that this

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:53.600
will be a good outcome
and things like that.

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:56.630
Or given a tweet, is
it a positive tweet

00:12:56.630 --> 00:12:58.500
or a negative tweet,
things like that.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:01.580
And then thirdly, we have
the prescriptive task.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:05.190
And these are a little more
uncommon at this point,

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:07.130
because this is
where you are saying,

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:12.060
I want to build a system that
will tell me what to do next.

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:15.110
And so that has some element
of almost like, if we're

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:18.000
playing a game of chess
or something, right?

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:20.030
What is the next
move I should make?

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:23.240
And this system should
be thinking about,

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:26.060
what is my opponent playing?

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:29.867
And what kind of options you
have and kind of gaming it out.

00:13:29.867 --> 00:13:31.700
So it's a little bit
of game theory in there

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:32.730
and things like that.

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:35.480
And so this is why it's a little
harder to do in the real world,

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:38.600
because such a system
would need so many inputs.

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:41.902
It can't just model the entire
known universe and be like, ah.

00:13:41.902 --> 00:13:42.860
The perfect next move--

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:43.700
[LAUGHS]

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:43.931
STEPHANIE WONG: If only.

00:13:43.931 --> 00:13:44.010
Yeah.

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:44.320
YUFUENG GUO: --is this.

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:45.060
Yeah, exactly.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:45.650
STEPHANIE WONG: All
right, so I want

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:47.270
to talk a little bit
about processing,

00:13:47.270 --> 00:13:49.603
because a lot of companies
are looking towards the cloud

00:13:49.603 --> 00:13:51.050
to do training and production.

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:54.950
Is this the only viable option
to really do it at that scale?

00:13:54.950 --> 00:13:57.770
Or is there an opportunity
for hybrid approach?

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:00.620
YUFUENG GUO: When you look
at it from the perspective

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:03.920
of your data set, if you
have a huge data set,

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:06.860
and you don't have any way
of really storing it on-prem,

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:09.090
and there's more data
coming in all the time,

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:10.240
there's only one answer.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:10.740
Right?

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:14.210
But in a lot of situations
today, maybe you're

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:16.790
only interested in doing machine
learning on a certain slice

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:18.050
of data, on a certain subset.

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:21.140
And there, it might make
sense to do training locally,

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:24.580
and then deploy that to
the cloud alongside all

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:26.520
of your other applications.

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:28.910
There's a number of
different ways to slice

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:32.090
and dice the different
workflows in the cloud,

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:35.990
but it basically boils down
to self-service and managed

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:36.800
services.

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:38.570
And so a self-serve
situation would

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:42.470
be your classic make a
VM, configure it yourself,

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:44.720
put whatever you want on
it, run your training.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.890
Now, maybe you put it
in a Kubernetes cluster

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:49.040
if you need more machines,
things like that.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:50.540
And then on the
other hand, you have

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:54.410
managed services which are more
along the lines of putting up

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:56.450
a Python package or
putting up your code,

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:58.910
and then having this
man or service take care

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:03.890
of the provisioning, and set up,
and tear down of these machines

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:06.710
after they finish running your
job, running your training job,

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:09.170
and output that
exported model file.

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:11.260
In terms of thinking
about which one to do,

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:13.670
I think the big one
I've seen come up

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:16.360
in terms of why would you want
to serve a machine learning

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:18.150
model locally?

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:20.990
And that answer typically
is along the lines

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:23.040
of response time and latency.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:27.290
So sometimes, you have a model
that goes on a mobile device,

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:29.630
and it needs to work
either more quickly,

00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:32.030
or work in situations
with no connectivity.

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:33.980
And so then you would
shrink that model down,

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:37.790
put it on your phone, and
embed it into your application.

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:39.740
On the other side of
things, why would you

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:41.870
keep it on a local server
in your own data center?

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:44.900
Maybe you need to
be close proximity.

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:48.410
You need to be close to wherever
you're communicating to.

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:52.010
Typically that shows up in some
kind of financial situations

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:54.740
where you need to make kind
of millisecond decisions,

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.693
and these predictions are only
good for the next slight window

00:15:57.693 --> 00:15:59.860
of time before, and the
markets change all the time.

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:02.318
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, so it
makes me think of stock market.

00:16:02.318 --> 00:16:04.170
And then on the mobile
side, IoT use cases.

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:05.170
YUFUENG GUO: Absolutely.

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:05.570
Yeah.

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:06.800
STEPHANIE WONG: So one thing
I've been curious about

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:08.880
was, how long training
needed to take,

00:16:08.880 --> 00:16:11.240
and how many iterations does
a team need to go through

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:13.190
before achieving an
optimal model that they

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:14.870
can deploy in production?

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:18.440
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah, this is kind
of the quintessential question

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:20.970
about machine learning.

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:24.080
You can train a small
model on a moderate sized

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:27.210
data set in seconds, a
low number of minutes.

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:29.150
But if you have a
big data set, and you

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:33.890
have really high requirements
of accuracy or performance,

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:37.610
it might take weeks
even to train a model.

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:39.440
Some of these huge
research models

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:42.470
that are really pushing the
state of the art, they're

00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:46.610
literally training
on hundreds of GPUs,

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:48.470
pushing petaflops of power--

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:48.830
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, wow.

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:50.810
YUFUENG GUO: --and doing
this for weeks on end.

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:50.990
STEPHANIE WONG: That's wild.

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:51.690
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah.

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:53.450
And at the end, you have a
model, and it's like, oh,

00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:54.908
I hoped that worked,
because I just

00:16:54.908 --> 00:16:57.890
burned all of this
computer and time,

00:16:57.890 --> 00:17:00.050
hoping to get
something out of that.

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:03.290
But yeah, it really comes down
to what are you optimizing for?

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:06.109
How much do you care about
those accuracy metrics?

00:17:06.109 --> 00:17:08.780
In a situation where maybe
you're making recommendations

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:12.170
on a website, you're
shopping, and it says, hey,

00:17:12.170 --> 00:17:14.660
some other products that
are similar to this product.

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:17.790
If one of them is a little
bit off or looks weird,

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:19.040
it's not the end of the world.

00:17:19.040 --> 00:17:21.457
STEPHANIE WONG: What's at stake
here is the real question.

00:17:21.457 --> 00:17:23.359
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah, exactly.

00:17:23.359 --> 00:17:25.160
But on the other
hand, in situations

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:28.970
like self-driving car
or a medical diagnosis,

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:31.670
these are literal life
and death decisions.

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:34.700
And so having a model that
is reliable performance

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:37.470
and accurate is way,
way more important,

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:38.957
and the stakes are much higher.

00:17:38.957 --> 00:17:40.790
The self-driving car
use case is another one

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:42.240
where latency matters.

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:45.518
And so that's why you are not
going to see cars == signaling

00:17:45.518 --> 00:17:47.226
that back up to the
cloud, be like, well,

00:17:47.226 --> 00:17:48.627
should I turn left
or right here?

00:17:48.627 --> 00:17:50.960
STEPHANIE WONG: You might run
into a wall at that point.

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:51.530
YUFUENG GUO: Exactly.

00:17:51.530 --> 00:17:53.572
STEPHANIE WONG: So what
are our options at Google

00:17:53.572 --> 00:17:55.970
to move from dev to prod
and have it work reliably

00:17:55.970 --> 00:17:57.632
in production at that scale?

00:17:57.632 --> 00:17:59.090
YUFUENG GUO: One
of the services we

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:02.870
have is the ClouDiA
platforms prediction

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:04.460
kind of managed service.

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:07.730
And all you really need to do
is take your exported model,

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:12.802
say, here it is, give it a
name, and you're basically done.

00:18:12.802 --> 00:18:14.510
And so it's pretty
magical in that sense,

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:16.560
because it's an auto
scaling service.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:19.160
So you don't have to worry about
provisioning infrastructure

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:23.210
responding to spikes in demand,
maybe during the holidays,

00:18:23.210 --> 00:18:25.110
during weekends,
things like that.

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:27.980
And so it'll automatically
scale up, and then

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:31.310
automatically scale back
down when traffic dies off,

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:33.320
and all you have to
do is finish training

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:36.430
your model and kind of tossed
it over the wall in some ways.

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:38.840
I know it's said a lot that,
you don't want to just toss

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:40.423
the model over the
wall from your data

00:18:40.423 --> 00:18:42.980
science team to kind of
your productionization team.

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:46.490
But if your model is being
deployed in production

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:49.190
by a service, and the data
scientists can basically

00:18:49.190 --> 00:18:52.070
just do that themselves,
it's like one command,

00:18:52.070 --> 00:18:54.860
then it kind of changes
the game in terms of where

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:55.952
you can spend your time.

00:18:55.952 --> 00:18:57.410
You can focus on
getting good data,

00:18:57.410 --> 00:18:58.880
getting good training outputs.

00:18:58.880 --> 00:19:00.560
And then once that's
done and you're

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:04.350
ready to push it to production,
it's not like this onerous task

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:06.470
that you then have
to get another team

00:19:06.470 --> 00:19:09.390
to handle and coordinate with
the modeling team and things

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:09.890
like that.

00:19:09.890 --> 00:19:10.510
So it's really nice.

00:19:10.510 --> 00:19:12.510
STEPHANIE WONG: I'm seeing
that as a commonality

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:13.805
for cloud in general.

00:19:13.805 --> 00:19:15.680
We talked a little bit
about managed services

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:17.040
versus custom modeling.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.280
And I know we have varying
options at Google as well.

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:20.540
Can you dive into that?

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:20.780
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah.

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:22.490
I mean, it all comes
down to your comfort

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:25.850
with kind of building, running,
and maintaining these systems

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:29.150
yourself and the expertise
you have in-house as well

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:31.700
as when you think
about do you want

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:34.100
this to be kind of an
investment in the long term?

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:37.220
Or is it something where you
wanted to just kind of work

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:39.370
in like had it be good enough?

00:19:39.370 --> 00:19:42.170
Managed services, things
like AutoML, they're

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:45.230
going to be necessarily
a little bit more

00:19:45.230 --> 00:19:48.920
constrained, because you can't
have them be fully customized.

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:51.260
That's the nature, by
definition, of this comparison.

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:52.760
STEPHANIE WONG: I
think that there's

00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:55.130
the key point here for
those who don't necessarily

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:58.110
have the right skill sets,
or they don't have the time,

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:01.250
then that's a great option is to
utilize machine learning APIs.

00:20:01.250 --> 00:20:04.250
And then even for those who
have experience with TensorFlow,

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:06.140
and I know TensorFlow
2.0 is out now.

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:08.300
But we have
libraries that people

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:09.950
can leverage instead
of reinventing

00:20:09.950 --> 00:20:12.175
the wheel in creating a
model from the get go.

00:20:12.175 --> 00:20:13.050
YUFUENG GUO: Exactly.

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:14.020
STEPHANIE WONG: OK, so I
want to talk about some

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:15.760
of the amazing use
cases, because I

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:18.200
know you've worked with
many companies in the past.

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:21.400
So industry use cases in
the medical field, IoT

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:24.043
that are really utilizing
the advancements in machine

00:20:24.043 --> 00:20:25.710
learning and some of
the Google tooling.

00:20:25.710 --> 00:20:27.127
YUFUENG GUO: Two
that come to mind

00:20:27.127 --> 00:20:29.425
is, one is the kind
of advancements

00:20:29.425 --> 00:20:30.550
on the medical side, right?

00:20:30.550 --> 00:20:32.530
Diabetic retinopathy
is kind of this leading

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:36.430
cause of blindness, and
Google has made huge strides

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:41.560
in improving the diagnosis of
diabetic retinopathy, which

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:45.700
of helps prevent blindness
as a result, a kind of side

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:47.240
effect of diabetes.

00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:50.470
And so that effort is kind of
ongoing, and the work they've

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:51.880
done in the kind
of publications,

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:54.280
for me, it was the first time
I'd seen a computer science

00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:58.600
paper get published in JAMA,
in the Journal of the American

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:00.070
Medical Association,
which is like,

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:02.140
when does computer
science get to publish

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:04.000
in the medical journal?

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:05.770
So that was really cool.

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:07.990
On the other kind
of use case, I got

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:10.870
to work a little bit with
Rainforest Connection,

00:21:10.870 --> 00:21:13.000
and they're a nonprofit
that kind of helps prevent

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:14.670
illegal logging in the Amazon.

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:17.710
And they have these devices
called Guardians out

00:21:17.710 --> 00:21:21.550
in the forest, and
they're listening.

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:24.497
They're listening for the sound
of trees being cut down, right?

00:21:24.497 --> 00:21:25.330
Because they listen.

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:25.890
STEPHANIE WONG: Wow.

00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:27.850
YUFUENG GUO: So then they have
all of this audio data coming

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:29.267
in, and they need
to process that,

00:21:29.267 --> 00:21:31.480
and they need to pick out
from there and recognize,

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:32.700
oh, there's sound from that.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:34.908
And then they can notify
law enforcement, right?

00:21:34.908 --> 00:21:36.700
And they can go out
and prevent that stuff.

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:37.570
STEPHANIE WONG:
That's really cool.

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:37.690
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah.

00:21:37.690 --> 00:21:38.190
STEPHANIE WONG: I
love that, yeah.

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:38.540
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah.

00:21:38.540 --> 00:21:40.510
So, and the other cool
thing about it is now,

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:41.380
they're taking that data.

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:42.550
They're saying, wow,
I have all this data.

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:45.100
And they're realizing what
else is in this data set.

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:48.790
They're hearing sounds of
animals, endangered species.

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:50.890
And they can start
using that information

00:21:50.890 --> 00:21:54.340
to find movement
patterns of animals

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:56.705
throughout the jungle
which is just wild.

00:21:56.705 --> 00:21:57.580
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:21:57.580 --> 00:21:59.643
OK, so we just covered
a lot of surface area

00:21:59.643 --> 00:22:01.060
when it comes to
machine learning.

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:03.040
I mean, companies need
to think about having

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:04.960
enough for compute
and storage, the data

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:06.700
sets, the right
personnel, and then

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:10.250
constantly re-evaluating their
models as new data comes in.

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:13.750
So how can you kind
of digest all of this?

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:15.670
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah, it's
a real culture shift

00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:18.910
in terms of making a company
and business data aware

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:22.390
and using data in an
intelligent fashion.

00:22:22.390 --> 00:22:23.900
It's not going to
happen overnight,

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:26.740
but I think getting
started sooner than later

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:27.730
is really important.

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:30.340
And it's going to take
time, but it will really

00:22:30.340 --> 00:22:33.550
transform how a
business can really

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:35.080
take advantage of
the information

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:36.380
that it already has.

00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:37.963
STEPHANIE WONG: So
with all that being

00:22:37.963 --> 00:22:40.480
said, what can people do to
get value out of ML right now?

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:41.230
YUFUENG GUO: Yeah.

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:42.700
I mean, it really
depends on kind

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:44.830
of where you're at in
terms of your experience

00:22:44.830 --> 00:22:46.150
and what you already know.

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:50.350
But you can kind of think
about it in terms of,

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:53.140
do you want to do manage
services and APIs,

00:22:53.140 --> 00:22:55.682
where you can get started just
understanding and digesting

00:22:55.682 --> 00:22:57.640
some of these high-level
concepts about machine

00:22:57.640 --> 00:22:59.890
learning and putting data
in and seeing what comes out

00:22:59.890 --> 00:23:01.670
the other side, so to speak?

00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:04.310
And if you already have
some familiarity with that,

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:05.860
